  "conclusions": [
    "Collision is independently reproduced by analytic physical sphere/AABB geometry.",
    "First pre-appearance reference passes through the later obstacle; the second published reference has positive sampled physical clearance.",
    "Native trajectory abort was observed, and the actuator adapter commanded braking/reversal; the stop was not ignored.",
    "At the second abort, remaining arm clearance was smaller than the ideal base braking distance in the shared bounded plant. Continued braking travel and the stopped arm reference explain the observed contact more directly than a large arm tracking error."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "This is the native REMANI-derived isolated simulation build with the declared sphere model and common lagged/acceleration-bounded base/arm plant; it is not a result for upstream unmodified REMANI or a real robot.",
    "Clearance checks are sampled physical sphere checks, not continuous mesh collision certification.",
    "The repaired reference local refinement uses 5 ms phase samples only in [3.1,3.5] seconds; positive sampled clearance is not continuous proof.",
    "Tracking estimates align actual state with the latest recorded controller progress; raw individual car_cmd and desired joint command streams were not separately logged.",
    "The native adapter retains the prior standby pose/vxy and q/dq controller and does not reproduce a hardware servo. Its omission of angular feedforward is an execution-model limitation; the observed maximum yaw error here was only about 0.18 degrees.",
    "The constant-deceleration stopping-distance comparison is explanatory; coupled sphere motion and the bounded lagged plant determine the actual event."
  ]
